WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:16.432
MH: Hi, my name is Melissa Hendershott and I'm a graduate student at UCSF.

00:00:16.433 --> 00:00:20.299
Today I'm here with Jim Farrell who is a professor at Stanford.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:23.066
He is also a member of several rock bands and today he's going

00:00:23.067 --> 00:00:26.966
to talk about his experiences in science AND in music.

00:00:26.967 --> 00:00:29.232
When did you first start playing music?

00:00:29.233 --> 00:00:34.066
JF: 1964, right in the Beatles invasion.

00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:38.466
I had this friend who had a ukulele, and who taught me how to play the ukulele.

00:00:38.467 --> 00:00:44.232
MH: How have you managed to integrate music into your life outside of science

00:00:44.233 --> 00:00:47.399
and sort of fit that into all your scientific endeavors?

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:53.732
JF: It's hard to fit it into either normal life or into scientific life,

00:00:53.733 --> 00:01:05.066
because it takes time; other things suffer. But you get pleasure out of it. And it's a good thing.

00:01:05.067 --> 00:04:01.099
Song: "Everybody is suffering"

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:04.066
MH: So did you play music all throughout your graduate career?

00:04:04.067 --> 00:04:11.466
JF: Not quite all. I showed up at medical school presuming that I was entering

00:04:11.467 --> 00:04:19.499
this serious grown up world where I would do nothing but study my histology and pathology.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:24.332
And I was pretty serious for about two years,

00:04:24.333 --> 00:04:30.966
doing research and making a little bit of progress towards my PhD thesis.

00:04:30.967 --> 00:04:38.832
But then bad things happened in my second year. All the premises that

00:04:38.833 --> 00:04:44.699
my PhD thesis were based on turned out to be wrong. I didn't really

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:49.366
have a good idea of something to work on and some other bad things happened in my life,

00:04:49.367 --> 00:04:56.232
so I thought, you know, I'd just try this band thing one last time,

00:04:56.233 --> 00:05:01.599
as a way of maybe introducing some happiness back into my life.

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:06.866
And it stuck.

00:05:06.867 --> 00:05:08.832
MH: How did you meet your band mates?

00:05:08.833 --> 00:05:12.699
JF: Most of them I met back then when I was a graduate student.

00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:19.599
So I've known those people for a long long time. I met my wife back then.

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:24.099
It was a really important decision as it turned out, this decision

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:34.899
to play in a band instead of devoting myself full time to my scholarly pursuits.

00:05:34.900 --> 00:07:33.132
song: "I like your eyes"

00:07:33.133 --> 00:07:36.666
MH: Can you tell us what some of the inspirations for your songs are?

00:07:36.667 --> 00:07:41.599
JF: Well, let's see, "Everybody is suffering" was about the fact that

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:47.132
everybody I knew at that point seemed to be having big trouble

00:07:47.133 --> 00:07:52.266
in their lives, but I was ok. Let's see, "Two Lindas" was about

00:07:52.267 --> 00:07:55.866
this friend of mine who was dating two women named Linda

00:07:55.867 --> 00:07:57.899
and love had not worked out for him.

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:06.032
I was hoping it would work out this time. And "I like your eyes" was about my wife

00:08:06.033 --> 00:08:14.099
actually, because I liked her eyes. So you know just basic songs

00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:19.566
saying true facts in one line after another.

00:08:19.567 --> 00:08:22.999
MH: Could you tell us about some of the venues that your band has played at?

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:27.732
JF: Oh, we've played the biggest! Maybe the biggest being the

00:08:27.733 --> 00:08:32.266
Department of Chemical and Systems Biology’s annual retreat.

00:08:32.267 --> 00:08:36.166
It was fantastic, especially when I was department chair.

00:08:36.167 --> 00:08:40.332
We could make the people dance, especially the young faculty.

00:08:40.333 --> 00:08:46.466
If they wouldn't dance – no tenure!

00:08:46.467 --> 00:11:54.433
song: "Two Lindas"

